// Plan-set ingestion, the job runner half: one job per uploaded plan set, one
// task per page per stage, and a claim column that makes a three-hundred-page
// run resumable. Diego's lane (AI, from 2026-09-26).
//
// Part of the multi-file Prisma schema in this folder.
//
// WHY A RUNNER AT ALL, AND WHY IT SHIPS BEFORE ANY INGESTION. A 300-sheet
// drawing set is 300 model calls. A Vercel function has 60 seconds. So the work
// cannot be one request, and the moment it is more than one request the only
// questions that matter are what happens when an invocation dies halfway and
// how a person sees where it got to. Those are answered here, with a stage that
// does no model work at all, so that the answers are proved on something cheap
// before three hundred paid calls depend on them.
//
// WHY HAND-ROLLED RATHER THAN A QUEUE. Both properties it needs already exist
// in this repo, proven, and neither needs a service: `notification-run.ts` has
// the bounded-run loop (45s of work under a 60s `maxDuration`, an injected
// `now()` so the budget is testable without waiting for it, and a TOTAL
// ordering so a truncated run cuts in the same place every time), and
// `allowance.ts` has claim-before-work. A queue would add a credential and a
// second failure mode to get the same two things.

/// Which pass over the pages a task belongs to.
///
/// AN ENUM RATHER THAN FREE TEXT, for the reason #526 paid for: a typo'd
/// stage string would create tasks nothing ever claims, and a job stuck at
/// "99%" with one invisible task left is indistinguishable from a job that
/// finished. A member here cannot be misspelled.
///
/// DO NOT ASK THIS FILE WHICH STAGES ARE BUILT. Ask `STAGE_WORK` in
/// `apps/web/lib/plan-ingest/stages.ts`: it is a total `Record` over this enum,
/// so a member with no entry fails to compile, and `RUNNABLE_STAGES` is DERIVED
/// from it by filtering the nulls. A status written here is a copy that nothing
/// checks, and it has already been wrong.
///
/// It said, until 2026-09-28: `PAGE_INVENTORY` "is the whole of this PR", the
/// rest "declared now and unreachable until the pass that implements them
/// lands", and `CLASSIFY`/`TITLE_BLOCK`/`SHEET_INDEX` each "Not built". Every
/// word of that was true for #538 and the phrase "this PR" is what rotted it:
/// the pass it was waiting for is the one that added these lines' replacement.
/// A comment that says a capability is MISSING is the expensive direction,
/// because it is the one that stops the next person looking — `stages.ts:34`
/// had meanwhile been saying these nulls are "neither 'not got round to yet'",
/// and this file disagreed with it and lost, exactly as CLAUDE.md's
/// `Sidebar.tsx` entry describes.
///
/// WHY THE ENUM STILL CARRIES A MEMBER NOTHING RUNS. Unchanged from #538 and
/// still the reason: a stage added later is a migration plus a retrofit onto
/// every call site, where a declared-but-unused member costs nothing. What
/// changed is that two of them are now unused ON PURPOSE and permanently, which
/// is a different fact from unfinished. The argument for each is on `STAGE_WORK`
/// and, for the index, at the top of `lib/plan-ingest/sheetIndex.ts`.
enum PlanIngestStage {
  /// Per page: does it exist, does it carry selectable text, and — since the
  /// title-block pass — the rotation-corrected text of the title-block region,
  /// stored as `PlanSheetText`. Opens the PDF. Still NO model call, which is
  /// what lets it read ~100 pages in a cron tick.
  PAGE_INVENTORY
  /// Folded into `TITLE_BLOCK` and deliberately never built: reading the title
  /// block yields the sheet number, the title AND the discipline, so a separate
  /// classify stage would be a second paid model call per page for an answer the
  /// first one already returned.
  CLASSIFY
  /// Per page: read the stored title-block text and propose a sheet number,
  /// title, discipline, scale, revision and issue date. The only stage that
  /// calls a model, and it never opens the PDF — so a retry costs one model call
  /// and no parse.
  TITLE_BLOCK
  /// Computed at READ TIME and deliberately never a stage — ordering, duplicate
  /// numbers and what still needs a person are pure computation over rows that
  /// already exist. As a per-page job it would run once per sheet over the whole
  /// set, or need a one-task job whose `pageCount` disagreed with its task count
  /// by design. See `lib/plan-ingest/sheetIndex.ts`.
  SHEET_INDEX
}

/// One ingestion run over one uploaded plan set.
///
/// A SEPARATE MODEL FROM `TakeoffPlan` rather than columns on it, because a
/// plan set can be ingested more than once — a re-run after a failure, or a
/// later stage arriving after the first pass is long finished — and a run has
/// its own beginning, end and outcome. Columns on the plan would make the
/// second run overwrite the first one's history.
///
/// PROGRESS IS NOT STORED, and that is not an optimisation. This repo's rule is
/// that derived state is never stored, because a stored flag can disagree with
/// what it was derived from — and here the disagreement has a specific shape: a
/// `percentComplete` column written by a worker that then dies is wrong
/// forever, and it is wrong in the direction that makes a stuck job look
/// finished. `count(finishedAt != null) / count(*)` over the tasks cannot lie,
/// because the tasks are the work.
model PlanIngestJob {
  id     String      @id @default(cuid())
  planId String
  plan   TakeoffPlan @relation(fields: [planId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Denormalised from the plan so a worker can scope a claim without a join,
  /// and so usage attribution has the company without reading two tables.
  /// Safe to duplicate: a plan never changes company.
  companyId String
  company   Company @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])

  /// Which pass this run is performing. One job per stage per plan, so a
  /// later stage is a new job rather than a mutation of the finished one.
  stage PlanIngestStage

  /// How many pages this run was created for, recorded at creation.
  ///
  /// THE ONE COUNT THAT IS STORED, and the exception is deliberate: it is not
  /// derived from the tasks, it is the claim the CREATOR made about how many
  /// there should be. Keeping it lets `pageCount` be compared against
  /// `count(tasks)` — a job whose task rows went missing is otherwise a job
  /// that reports 100% having done part of the work, which is exactly the
  /// failure the no-stored-progress rule above is about. The two numbers
  /// disagreeing is a fact worth being able to see.
  pageCount Int

  /// Entered by the runner, not defaulted: a job row exists before any worker
  /// has picked it up, and `startedAt` must mean "a worker began" rather than
  /// "a row was written".
  startedAt  DateTime?
  /// Set when every task is finished or has exhausted its attempts. A job with
  /// failed tasks still FINISHES — it does not hang — and the failures are
  /// visible per page. A run that stopped short is a run nobody can retry.
  finishedAt DateTime?

  /// Who uploaded the set this run is for. Nullable because a run started by a
  /// scheduler or a backfill has no person behind it.
  startedByUserId String?
  startedByUser   User?   @relation(fields: [startedByUserId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  tasks PlanIngestTask[]

  /// What a `TITLE_BLOCK` run proposed, one row per page. Empty for every other
  /// stage — `PAGE_INVENTORY` writes `PlanSheetText`, which is keyed on the PLAN
  /// rather than on a run because the text of a page of an immutable file does not
  /// depend on which run read it.
  sheetProposals PlanSheetProposal[]

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// One run per stage per plan at a time. A second `PAGE_INVENTORY` job on the
  /// same plan while the first is unfinished would have two workers claiming
  /// two task sets over the same pages, and the progress figure would be over
  /// whichever set was queried.
  ///
  /// It is NOT unique on (planId, stage) outright, because re-running a stage
  /// after it finished is legitimate. `finishedAt` being part of the key is not
  /// expressible here, so the constraint the database can hold is the index,
  /// and `startPlanIngest` enforces the rest in a transaction.
  @@index([planId, stage])
  @@index([companyId, createdAt])
  @@index([startedByUserId])
}

/// One unit of claimable work: one page, one stage.
///
/// THE CLAIM COLUMN IS THE DESIGN. A worker takes work with `updateMany` on
/// `claimedAt: null`, which is atomic in Postgres — two workers racing for the
/// same row produce one winner and one zero-count update, so the loser simply
/// takes the next row. Nothing needs a lock, and no work is done twice.
///
/// WHAT MAKES IT RESUMABLE is that claiming and finishing are separate. An
/// invocation that dies mid-page leaves a row claimed and unfinished, which is
/// a state the next run can SEE — as opposed to a queue message that was acked
/// on receipt, where a dead worker's page is simply gone.
///
/// AND WHAT MAKES THAT SAFE RATHER THAN A NEW BUG is `claimExpiresAt` below.
/// Diego asked for stuck-lease reclaim specifically, and it is the correction
/// that turns this from a clever column into a correct one: without it a task
/// claimed by an invocation that then died is claimed FOREVER, and a job hangs
/// at one page short with no error and nothing to retry. A claim column with no
/// expiry converts a resumable job into a permanently stuck one, which is worse
/// than not being resumable at all — a stuck job looks like a slow job.
model PlanIngestTask {
  id    String        @id @default(cuid())
  jobId String
  job   PlanIngestJob @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// 1-based, the way the viewer and the title block both count — the same
  /// convention `TakeoffPlanPage.pageNumber` uses.
  ///
  /// A PAGE NUMBER, NOT A FOREIGN KEY to `TakeoffPlanPage`. Those rows are
  /// created by a PERSON naming a sheet in the viewer, so most pages of a
  /// freshly uploaded set have none — and ingestion's whole job is to run over
  /// pages nobody has named yet. A foreign key would make the work depend on
  /// the thing the work produces.
  pageNumber Int

  /// Repeated from the job so a claim can be scoped to one stage without a
  /// join, and so a task row means something on its own.
  stage PlanIngestStage

  /// When a worker took this row. Null means available.
  claimedAt DateTime?

  /// When the claim lapses, so another worker may take it.
  ///
  /// SET BY THE CLAIMER, not computed on read, so the value is what the worker
  /// that holds it actually promised — a deadline derived at read time from a
  /// constant would change meaning the moment somebody tuned the constant, and
  /// would apply retroactively to claims made under the old one.
  claimExpiresAt DateTime?

  /// Set when the work for this page succeeded. A finished task is never
  /// reclaimed, whatever its claim says.
  finishedAt DateTime?

  /// How many times this page has been attempted, incremented on CLAIM rather
  /// than on failure.
  ///
  /// ON CLAIM IS THE LOAD-BEARING CHOICE. Incrementing on failure cannot count
  /// the attempt that died before it could write anything — which is the exact
  /// failure mode a page that reliably kills its worker produces, and it would
  /// be retried forever at the ceiling's expense. Counting the claim means a
  /// page that crashes the process still exhausts its attempts and reports.
  attempts Int @default(0)

  /// Why the last attempt failed, for the per-page retry in the UI. A SENTENCE
  /// for a person, never a stack: this is rendered on a screen beside a Retry
  /// button.
  error String?

  /// The earliest a failed task may be claimed again. Null means now.
  ///
  /// ITS OWN COLUMN RATHER THAN DERIVED FROM `updatedAt`, which was the first
  /// design and is wrong: `updatedAt` moves for reasons that are not attempts,
  /// so backoff computed from it would lengthen every time anything touched the
  /// row, and a column that means two things cannot be reasoned about.
  ///
  /// WHY BACKOFF NEEDS A COLUMN AT ALL when the cron only ticks every few
  /// minutes — which is the objection worth answering, because the schedule
  /// does supply most of the delay for free. Two places it does not: a single
  /// invocation's loop can re-claim a task it just failed, within the same 45
  /// seconds, and burn the attempt ceiling on one page in one run; and the
  /// per-page Retry button is a person who can click faster than any sensible
  /// interval. Without this, "three attempts" can be spent in three seconds
  /// against a transient fault that would have cleared.
  nextAttemptAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// One task per page per stage per job. The guard against a double-create
  /// producing two claimable rows for one page, which would spend twice.
  @@unique([jobId, pageNumber, stage])
  /// The claim query's own index: available work for a job, oldest page first.
  @@index([jobId, finishedAt, claimedAt])
  /// Backoff's own index — the claim skips tasks whose retry is still ahead.
  @@index([nextAttemptAt])
  /// The reclaim query's index: expired claims across all jobs.
  @@index([claimExpiresAt])
}

/// WHAT PAGE N OF THIS PLAN SET SAYS — the evidence a proposal is made from.
///
/// TWO MODELS RATHER THAN ONE, AND THE ARCHITECTURE FORCES IT. `PlanIngestJob`
/// is one job per STAGE per plan, so `PAGE_INVENTORY` and `TITLE_BLOCK` are two
/// different job rows. A single model keyed on the ingest job would therefore put
/// the extracted text and the proposal made from it on different rows, which is
/// not a design, it is a bug. So the split follows what each one IS.
///
/// THIS ONE IS KEYED ON THE PLAN, and that is correct rather than convenient:
/// the text of page N of a PDF is a property of an IMMUTABLE FILE. Re-running
/// `PAGE_INVENTORY` reads the same bytes and writes the same words, so an upsert
/// here is idempotent and overwriting costs nothing. The proposal below cannot say
/// that about itself, which is why it is keyed differently.
///
/// AND IT IS STORED DERIVED STATE, DELIBERATELY. This schema's rule is that
/// derived state is never stored, and `TakeoffPlan` refuses a `pageCount` column
/// on exactly that ground. The distinction: a `pageCount` from the browser could
/// only ever hold a CLIENT'S CLAIM the server had no way to check, whereas this is
/// the server's own read of a file that cannot change. It is kept because it is
/// the EVIDENCE THE PROPOSAL WAS MADE FROM — without it `proposedReason` is a
/// sentence nobody can check, and `intake.prisma`'s standard for a reason is that
/// it must be checkable. It also means `TITLE_BLOCK` never re-opens the PDF: the
/// expensive stage reads rows, so a retry costs one model call and no parse.
model PlanSheetText {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// CASCADEs from the plan, which is what keeps this model out of
  /// `HANDLED_MODELS` and both cleanup `del()` orders — the trick `TakeoffPlan`'s
  /// own `jobId` comment documents. One `takeoffPlan.deleteMany({ jobId })`
  /// reaches it.
  planId String
  plan   TakeoffPlan @relation(fields: [planId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// 1-based, the way the viewer, the runner and the title block all count.
  pageNumber Int

  /// Whether there is a text layer to read at all.
  ///
  /// FALSE IS A FACT, NOT A FAILURE, and the difference is money. A scanned sheet
  /// has no vector text and never will, so a stage reporting it as `{ok: false}`
  /// would fail three times with backoff, spend three sheet claims, and leave a
  /// row whose Retry button can never succeed. A wholly scanned 300-page set
  /// would produce 900 claims and a 300-row retry list. So it is recorded here,
  /// the page is never sent to a model, and the review screen says this sheet
  /// needs its number typed in.
  hasTextLayer Boolean

  /// The title block's words, in reading order, or null when there is no text
  /// layer. What the model is given — never the file, never an image.
  titleBlockText String?

  /// Whether those words came from the title-block REGION or from the whole page.
  ///
  /// A sheet whose block sits somewhere the region filter does not expect would
  /// otherwise hand the model an almost-empty region, which is indistinguishable
  /// from a scan. Falling back to the whole page costs tokens and answers
  /// correctly. Recorded so a reader can see which happened, because a proposal
  /// made from a whole page deserves more scepticism than one made from the block.
  wholePageFallback Boolean @default(false)

  /// The sheet AS DISPLAYED, in points, and its own `/Rotate`. 72 points to the
  /// inch, so 2592 is a 36" sheet. Taken from the viewport rather than the
  /// MediaBox, so a rotated landscape sheet reports landscape — the distinction
  /// `planPdf.ts` exists to get right and the one a raw x/y filter got wrong.
  widthPt  Float
  heightPt Float
  rotation Int   @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  proposals PlanSheetProposal[]

  /// One row per page of a plan. Safe to upsert — see the header.
  @@unique([planId, pageNumber])
  @@index([planId, hasTextLayer])
}

/// WHAT THE MODEL READ OFF ONE TITLE BLOCK, and what a person said about it.
///
/// THE ROW IS THE PROPOSAL, NOT THE FILING — `intake.prisma`'s rule, copied
/// rather than re-argued. Accepting one records the acceptance HERE and writes no
/// `TakeoffPlanPage`, builds no sheet index elsewhere, and changes nothing a
/// quantity depends on. `lib/intake/review.ts` states why: "a label promising it
/// before it exists is how a demo becomes a lie."
///
/// KEYED ON THE RUN, NOT THE PAGE — the opposite of `PlanSheetText` above, and
/// the reason is re-running. A job may legitimately run `TITLE_BLOCK` again: a
/// better prompt, a newer model. Keyed on `(planId, pageNumber)` the second run
/// would OVERWRITE the first proposal, including one an estimator had already
/// accepted beside, and the pair of columns exists precisely so "how often did a
/// person change the answer" stays answerable. So this is APPEND-ONLY per run,
/// the shape `TakeoffScaleCalibration` already uses in `takeoff.prisma`:
/// "Recalibrating a sheet INSERTS a row; the newest row is the active one (derived
/// from `createdAt`, not stored)". The index reads the newest proposal per page.
model PlanSheetProposal {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// The run that produced it. CASCADEs, so deleting a run takes its proposals.
  ingestJobId String
  ingestJob   PlanIngestJob @relation(fields: [ingestJobId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Denormalised for the read — "the newest proposal for each page of this plan"
  /// is the query the index is built from, and it must not have to walk every run
  /// to answer it. Also the Cascade that keeps this out of the cleanup scripts.
  planId String
  plan   TakeoffPlan @relation(fields: [planId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  pageNumber Int

  /// The evidence this was read from, so a reviewer can see the words the
  /// proposal came out of rather than taking `proposedReason` on trust.
  sheetTextId String?
  sheetText   PlanSheetText? @relation(fields: [sheetTextId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// THE PROPOSAL, exactly as the model made it, at the moment it made it.
  ///
  /// STORED RATHER THAN RE-DERIVED, the same deliberate exception `intake.prisma`
  /// takes and for its reason: "a newer classifier would then quietly rewrite the
  /// reason a person was shown when they made their decision. A proposal is a
  /// thing that happened, like a `capturedAt`." Never updated after insert.
  ///
  /// Null means THE TITLE BLOCK DID NOT SAY, which is a real answer and distinct
  /// from an empty string. The prompt's first rule is never to invent a sheet
  /// number.
  proposedSheetNumber String?
  proposedTitle       String?
  proposedDiscipline  String?

  /// WHAT KIND OF DRAWING THIS PAGE IS — one of `SHEET_PAGE_TYPES` in
  /// `packages/integrations/src/planSheets.ts`: COVER, PLAN, ELEVATION, SECTION,
  /// DETAIL, SCHEDULE or OTHER.
  ///
  /// `proposedTitle` already holds what the sheet printed ("EXTERIOR
  /// ELEVATIONS"), and that is a label a person reads. This is the one the app
  /// can act on — "which pages are the schedules?" is the question the takeoff
  /// side needs answered, and four titles can mean one kind.
  ///
  /// A STRING RATHER THAN AN ENUM, deliberately, and for the opposite reason to
  /// the one that usually applies. This column holds MODEL OUTPUT. An enum
  /// column would reject an unexpected value at write time, and a Server Action
  /// that throws has its message REDACTED in production — so a model answering
  /// "FLOOR PLAN" would lose the whole proposal rather than one field.
  /// `normaliseSheetPageType` is the gate instead: it maps the words a title
  /// block actually prints onto the closed set and answers OTHER for anything
  /// else, so what reaches this column is always one of the seven.
  /// `sheetPageType.test.ts` is what holds that promise.
  ///
  /// Null means NOBODY HAS READ THIS PAGE YET, or the text gave nothing to judge
  /// from — distinct from OTHER, which means it is none of the six. A proposal
  /// written before prompt `plan-title-block.2` also holds null, because that
  /// reader did not return the field at all.
  proposedPageType    String?
  proposedScale       String?

  /// THE REVISION AND DATE PRINTED ON THIS SHEET, named so they cannot be
  /// mistaken for `TakeoffPlan.revisionLabel` and `sheetIssuedOn` — which are
  /// per-PLAN, ENTERED by a person, and what `lib/takeoff-currency.ts` runs on.
  /// NOTHING IN `takeoff-currency.ts` READS THESE, and nothing should: a
  /// model-proposed per-page date becoming a second source of "is this takeoff
  /// current" is how two surfaces start disagreeing about whether somebody may
  /// build from a drawing. Text, not a date, because a date on a drawing is
  /// characters until a person confirms them.
  titleBlockRevisionText  String?
  titleBlockIssueDateText String?

  /// The sentence shown in the review table, in words a person can check against
  /// the title block beside it — "Sheet number read from the lower right of the
  /// title block". Never "AI determined": a reason nobody can check is a reason
  /// nobody can overrule.
  proposedReason String

  proposedConfidence PlanSheetConfidence

  /// Which model and which prompt produced this. `AskUsage.promptVersion` has had
  /// no writer since it was added, and `docs/ai/DECISIONS.md` says the guard ships
  /// WITH the first prompt file rather than after it — this is that first prompt
  /// file. Together these two are what make a change in output attributable to a
  /// change we made rather than to the weather.
  model         String
  promptVersion String

  /// WHAT THE PERSON SAID IT WAS. Null until they accept.
  ///
  /// ONLY THE TWO FIELDS SOMETHING DOWNSTREAM CONSUMES ARE PAIRED, which is what
  /// `intake.prisma` actually does rather than what a first reading of it
  /// suggests: it pairs `proposedKind`/`acceptedKind`, the one value that drives
  /// an action, and leaves `revisionHint` and `jobHint` unpaired. Discipline,
  /// scale, revision and date are shown and correctable but unpaired for the same
  /// reason — six pairs would be twelve columns holding nulls forever, and the
  /// eval measures calibration against the title block, not against what somebody
  /// edited.
  acceptedSheetNumber String?
  acceptedTitle       String?

  status PlanSheetProposalStatus @default(PROPOSED)

  /// WHO accepted it and WHEN. `DocumentIntake` records neither and its own notes
  /// flag that as worth deciding consciously; decided the other way here, because
  /// a sheet number is a thing a GC may later dispute and "who said this sheet was
  /// A-101" is then worth being able to answer.
  acceptedByUserId String?
  acceptedBy       User?     @relation("PlanSheetProposalAcceptedBy", fields: [acceptedByUserId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  acceptedAt       DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// One proposal per page per run.
  @@unique([ingestJobId, pageNumber])
  /// The index's own query: the proposals for a plan, newest run first.
  @@index([planId, pageNumber, createdAt])
  @@index([planId, status])
}

/// How sure the reading was.
///
/// LOW SORTS TO THE TOP of the review list and HIGH to the bottom, which is
/// `DocumentIntakeConfidence`'s choice and its reasoning: it "is what makes
/// over-claiming it the expensive mistake". An honest "I am not sure" is the
/// correct answer, and the eval grades calibration as well as correctness because
/// a sheet number that is 85% right and reads as certain is a wrong bid.
enum PlanSheetConfidence {
  HIGH
  MEDIUM
  LOW
}

/// A rejected proposal STAYS, so the same set ingested again is not proposed
/// afresh as though nobody had looked at it — `intake.prisma`'s reason for keeping
/// a DISMISSED row.
enum PlanSheetProposalStatus {
  PROPOSED
  ACCEPTED
  REJECTED
}
